## Terms

```
'manager'               the program of Controllers of ManagerDesign
'manager process'       a process running the manager program
'launcher'              an executable file of the program folder of the manager; the 'launcher' of a manager process is the one that started that process
'manager folder'        the folder where one version of the manager keeps projects.info and the other files of ManagerDesign; managerFolder(l) is the 'manager folder' of the 'launcher' l
'single' manager        the manager process holding the lock of the 'manager folder' after its first manager.reconcile; at most one on a desk
'manager GUI'           the program of ManagerGuiDesign, started by ManagerGui.start
'portable Fearless'     the program of Coordinator that compiles and runs the project folder given as its argument
registered project      a project of projects.info
project folder          the folder of a registered project, the path of that project in projects.info
'compilation cache'     the folder .fearless_out of a project folder; it belongs to the Coordinator, whatever the kind of the project
'mains chain'           of a project p: the mains of one Run of p, or the one main of one OpenWithOrShortcut, run one after the other, from the start of the first main to the end of the last one
'compilation'           of a project p: from the start of Coordinator.compile of p to the end of manager.compile(p)
'busy'                  p is 'busy' while a 'mains chain' or a 'compilation' of p runs, or a 'mains chain' of a project q with (t->p) in q.mounts runs
busyProjects            the 'busy' projects
'invalid'               p is 'invalid' when invalid(p) of ManagerDesign
'out of date'           p is 'out of date' when state(p) == outOfDate of ManagerDesign
Output                  of a project p: what the requests about p write, with the marks of the starts and the ends of the mains and of the 'compilation'
```

## Notation

```
manager /data/hello             a command line: it opens a definition of what running it does
manager.send(m)                 a method: it opens a definition of what calling it does
A, B, C, ...                    the lines of a definition or of a story, in order, each one after the end of the line before
```
Several definitions of the same command line or method are the ways it can go; the first lines of each definition say which way.
A block of lettered lines with no command line and no method before it is a story: one case, told line by line.
Inside a definition or a story, a line `manager.x(...)`, `Coordinator.x(...)`, `Reconciliator.x(...)` or `ManagerGui.x(...)` calls that definition; a call of a definition that ends the manager is the last line.
A line "Unlike X, Y" says what one may expect, X, and what happens instead, Y, with the reason.
```
p.m                             the main m of the project p: hello.greet.Hello
(m) in p.mains                  m is a selected main of p
(t->d) in p.mounts              the data project d is mounted on the DataRead type t of p
(a) in p.m.attenuators          a is an attenuator of the main p.m
p.m.out+="Foo"                  the main p.m writes Foo in the Output of p
p.chain                         the 'mains chain' of p
p.compilation                   the 'compilation' of p
x started, x ended              told by the manager to the manager GUI: x started, x ended
x ::= y | z                     a grammar: x is y or z; ? is optional, * is any number; text after a grammar line, on that line, constrains it
f(x) = y   if c                 a function: f(x) is y when c holds
Foo                             a program outside the Fearless family
```
The user observes the icon of a file on the desktop and in the file explorer, and the icon of a window in every place where the desk shows the windows of the running programs.

## Reading a design

The definitions and the stories specify the behaviour, not the implementation: an implementation may take the steps in another order, or in another way, when no person and no program can tell the difference, also after a crash.
An implementation decides alone every detail a design leaves open below its concepts: wire formats, encodings, intervals, the library calls, the content of the files a design names (information for Eclipse can be added to `manager.info`).
An implementation never adds what the user or another program can observe and the design does not describe: a file, a verb, a GUI element, a message, a fallback. Those go into the design first.
The Implementation notes of a design say what an implementation needs that neither the design nor the repositories say.
